WEBVTT

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.347
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Part 2

00:00:02.647 --> 00:00:05.786
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> When was the last time you saw a production of a Shakespeare play yourself?

00:00:06.392 --> 00:00:13.766
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> I have to confess that I haven’t been to see a Shakespeare play recently, actually.

00:00:13.825 --> 00:00:14.645
Well, the most recent one was

00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:18.115
– I went to see some students at Central School for Speech and Drama, actually,

00:00:18.089 --> 00:00:20.180
so I did go and see Macbeth recently.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:26.212
So, really, you know, they were really into it and really relishing it…

00:00:26.365 --> 00:00:37.743
they did a bit of gender-blind performing, where they had a lot of the women playing male roles;

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:40.148
they actually didn’t have any of the men playing female roles,

00:00:40.308 --> 00:00:45.638
which I thought was an interesting choice, that that was weighted in one direction.

00:00:46.458 --> 00:00:49.450
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> What do you think in general about the trend in modern productions

00:00:49.543 --> 00:00:53.946
for male roles to be played by women, for example, Glenda Jackson as King Lear?

00:00:54.873 --> 00:00:57.676
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> I think, you know, theatre needs to be kept alive and fresh,

00:00:57.701 --> 00:00:59.344
you need to keep experimenting.

00:00:59.877 --> 00:01:08.455
The thing that concerns me is if you’re doing it a) because it’s a gimmick, or b) because it’s kind of trendy.

00:01:08.989 --> 00:01:14.941
At the same time, I sympathize with, you know, strong, experienced women actors,

00:01:15.327 --> 00:01:22.302
because they don’t have the choice of powerful roles that men do if you’re playing according to gender,

00:01:22.723 --> 00:01:29.228
so I think it’s great to give women actors the opportunity to play these hefty roles that Shakespeare

00:01:29.406 --> 00:01:31.708
– that are written for male characters in Shakespeare.

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:34.715
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> What about male actors playing female roles?

00:01:34.935 --> 00:01:39.434
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> I think it’s all about the actor, isn’t it, and whether the role suits them, so…

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:42.698
You know, there is a slight political agenda there, of,

00:01:42.785 --> 00:01:47.415
well, why have men taken even more roles away from women – question mark

00:01:47.888 --> 00:01:49.938
– when women are struggling enough?

00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:54.764
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> How would you feel about a male actor playing a female role in one of your plays,

00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:55.717
or vice versa?

00:01:55.877 --> 00:02:01.782
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> I have no problem with men playing women or women playing men in any of my plays.

00:02:01.849 --> 00:02:03.645
One of my plays, Poppy and George,

00:02:03.705 --> 00:02:12.447
is all about a woman who lives her life as a man because she feels too limited to live her life,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.124
if you like, in women’s clothes in the world in 1919 just after the First World War.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:22.071
So, in a way it’s a theme of my work that, you know,

00:02:22.331 --> 00:02:28.010
the theme of the roles we play in the world, and do they restrict us or do they liberate us?

00:02:28.563 --> 00:02:36.256
In terms of, yeah, theatre, I mean, the things that I’ve found difficult

00:02:36.296 --> 00:02:42.539
when I’ve seen productions are usually around feeling that an actor isn’t right for the role.

00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:50.148
That isn’t about… you know, you can have a person of the gender the character is,

00:02:50.708 --> 00:02:57.285
or what the character is, and they – and yet, they’re just not the right person.

00:02:57.372 --> 00:03:06.265
So, I think what makes a person be able to bring a role alive fully is about, I suppose,

00:03:06.298 --> 00:03:11.043
their empathy, their connection with the character and the themes of the play,

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.973
and their skill probably, if we’re talking about professional work.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.097
So, it’s those things that matter most, I reckon.

00:03:18.737 --> 00:03:21.707
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Your play <i>Kindertransport</i> is a set text in schools.

00:03:22.041 --> 00:03:26.091
Do you think students can enjoy it just from reading it, or do they need to see a performance?

00:03:29.327 --> 00:03:34.186
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> I have talked to many students and visited many schools

00:03:34.253 --> 00:03:40.969
who study <i>Kindertransport</i> and the thing I find I do when I go in that often teachers aren’t doing

00:03:41.557 --> 00:03:46.419
– if it’s in an English literature context, this is, ’cause it’s also studied in a drama context,

00:03:46.444 --> 00:03:49.632
where of course their approach to it is to perform it.

00:03:49.705 --> 00:03:54.618
So, there’s loads of students who study <i>Kindertransport</i> to actually mount a production of it,

00:03:54.672 --> 00:03:58.176
or to mount a, you know, a series of performances of extracts.

00:03:58.243 --> 00:04:05.663
So, in a sense, that feels to me to be the most rewarding way to study the play.

00:04:05.689 --> 00:04:15.467
It isn’t – <i>Kindertransport</i> isn’t really, in itself, if you like, a work of literature.

00:04:15.753 --> 00:04:20.610
So, you only touch the tip of the iceberg, just reading it, and I guess that’s true of Shakespeare,

00:04:20.676 --> 00:04:22.311
you know, in answer to the earlier questions.

00:04:22.618 --> 00:04:26.571
You’re not getting everything out of the play, which is maybe what Helen Mirren means.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:30.191
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Diane Samuels, thank you very much, it’s been a pleasure.